  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Better Health Outcomes through Mentoring and Assessment (BHOMA II)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In Zambia, levels of illness and death remain unacceptably high. 440 per 100,000 women die in childbirth every year, compared with 12 per 100,000 in the UK. Almost one in ten children die before their fifth birthday, and a third of these deaths happen in the first month of life. Life expectancy is just 48 years. Most deaths are preventable, but the public health system is poorly staffed and resourced, leading to low uptake of primary health services. This project builds on a programme to reduce death rates in adults and children through improvements in primary health care, which has been running since 2010. The programme seeks to improve the quality of care in health facilities in three rural districts of Lusaka province, and to improve the demand for and use of health services. Funding from Comic Relief will enable the introduction of strengthened maternal health and new-born care, and will add another year to the life of the programme, which will help improve the quality of learning. Health worker training, mentoring and support will improve the supply of health services and community-level activities will promote the demand for them. This will lead to improved maternal and newborn health and fewer people dying from preventable illnesses.</narrative>
  </description>
